1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,746
A folded umbrella.

2
00:00:01,746 --> 00:00:02,750
A paper fan.

3
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:03,802
A flower bud.

4
00:00:04,202 --> 00:00:07,338
Each one packs something
big into a small space.

5
00:00:07,338 --> 00:00:09,304
The umbrella packs its cloth.

6
00:00:09,304 --> 00:00:11,073
The bud packs its petals.

7
00:00:11,523 --> 00:00:13,309
Aarav loves puzzles like this.

8
00:00:13,309 --> 00:00:17,359
He is aiming for JEE, and he cut a
lady's finger open in the kitchen.

9
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:20,051
The seeds sat in neat
rows, in little rooms.

10
00:00:20,051 --> 00:00:21,663
Who packed them so neatly?

11
00:00:22,013 --> 00:00:24,122
A flower solves two packing puzzles.

12
00:00:24,122 --> 00:00:28,604
How to fold its petals in the bud, and
how to pack its ovules in the ovary.

13
00:00:29,104 --> 00:00:33,524
By the end, you will name four ways
of folding and five ways of packing,

14
00:00:33,524 --> 00:00:35,785
and spot each one in a real plant.

15
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:37,903
First, two new words.

16
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:40,792
A ring of flower parts is called a whorl.

17
00:00:40,792 --> 00:00:42,446
The sepals make one whorl.

18
00:00:42,446 --> 00:00:43,957
The petals make another.

19
00:00:44,407 --> 00:00:48,267
Aestivation is how the sepals or
petals sit in a flower bud, compared to

20
00:00:48,267 --> 00:00:50,252
their neighbours in the same whorl.

21
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:51,371
Who overlaps whom?

22
00:00:51,821 --> 00:00:54,976
Placentation is how the ovules
are arranged inside the ovary.

23
00:00:54,976 --> 00:00:57,679
Ovules are the tiny bodies
that later become seeds.

24
00:00:58,129 --> 00:01:01,271
They sit on a cushion of
tissue called the placenta.

25
00:01:01,271 --> 00:01:03,930
So placentation asks:
where is the cushion?

26
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,225
Let's start with the bud.

27
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:10,812
Imagine cutting a bud straight across,
and looking down at its five petals.

28
00:01:11,162 --> 00:01:12,062
Valvate.

29
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:14,019
The petals just touch at their edges.

30
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:15,602
No petal overlaps another.

31
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:16,637
Like Calotropis.

32
00:01:17,387 --> 00:01:18,287
Twisted.

33
00:01:18,086 --> 00:01:23,142
Each petal overlaps the next one, all
in the same direction, like a pinwheel.

34
00:01:23,142 --> 00:01:25,611
China rose, lady's finger and cotton.

35
00:01:26,361 --> 00:01:27,261
Imbricate.

36
00:01:27,146 --> 00:01:30,130
The edges overlap, but
in no fixed direction.

37
00:01:30,130 --> 00:01:33,308
Some petals sit fully
outside, some fully inside.

38
00:01:33,308 --> 00:01:34,693
Cassia and gulmohur.

39
00:01:35,443 --> 00:01:36,343
Vexillary.

40
00:01:36,246 --> 00:01:39,712
Pea and bean flowers have
five petals of three kinds.

41
00:01:39,712 --> 00:01:43,110
The largest, the standard,
overlaps the two wings.

42
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:47,230
The wings overlap the two smallest
petals, which form the keel.

43
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:52,733
Vexillary is also called papilionaceous,
which means butterfly like.

44
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:54,599
Now the second puzzle.

45
00:01:54,599 --> 00:01:56,931
An ovary is built from
units called carpels.

46
00:01:56,931 --> 00:01:58,611
Watch where the ovules end up.

47
00:01:58,961 --> 00:01:59,861
Marginal.

48
00:01:59,711 --> 00:02:01,951
One carpel folds like a pea pod.

49
00:02:01,951 --> 00:02:06,589
The placenta forms a ridge along one
seam, and the ovules sit in two rows

50
00:02:06,589 --> 00:02:07,489
on it.

51
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:08,150
Like pea.

52
00:02:08,809 --> 00:02:09,709
Axile.

53
00:02:09,382 --> 00:02:12,945
Several carpels join, and
their walls meet in the middle.

54
00:02:12,945 --> 00:02:17,100
The placenta is on the central
axis, and the ovary has many rooms.

55
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:19,069
China rose, tomato and lemon.

56
00:02:19,819 --> 00:02:20,719
Parietal.

57
00:02:20,456 --> 00:02:23,139
The ovules grow on the
inner wall of the ovary.

58
00:02:23,139 --> 00:02:27,164
It has one room, but in mustard and
Argemone, a false wall splits it into

59
00:02:27,164 --> 00:02:28,064
two.

60
00:02:28,342 --> 00:02:29,326
Free central.

61
00:02:29,326 --> 00:02:31,110
There are no walls inside.

62
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:33,445
The ovules sit on a central column.

63
00:02:33,445 --> 00:02:34,921
Dianthus and primrose.

64
00:02:35,671 --> 00:02:36,571
Basal.

65
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:41,040
The placenta grows at the base of
the ovary, and holds just one ovule.

66
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:42,641
Sunflower and marigold.

67
00:02:43,391 --> 00:02:45,041
Two pairs get mixed up.

68
00:02:45,041 --> 00:02:46,943
Let's put them side by side.

69
00:02:47,293 --> 00:02:49,620
Twisted and imbricate both overlap.

70
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:54,711
In twisted, every petal overlaps the
next, one way round, like china rose.

71
00:02:54,711 --> 00:02:58,394
In imbricate, there is no
fixed order, like gulmohur.

72
00:02:59,144 --> 00:03:02,571
Axile and free central both
keep the ovules in the middle.

73
00:03:02,571 --> 00:03:06,223
In axile, walls divide the
ovary into rooms, like a tomato.

74
00:03:06,223 --> 00:03:09,830
In free central, there are no
walls at all, like Dianthus.

75
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:11,480
Your turn.

76
00:03:11,437 --> 00:03:12,857
Cut a lemon across.

77
00:03:12,857 --> 00:03:17,373
Walls divide it into segments, and
the seeds sit near the centre, where

78
00:03:17,373 --> 00:03:18,536
the walls meet.

79
00:03:18,536 --> 00:03:20,104
Axile or free central?

80
00:03:20,104 --> 00:03:21,366
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:24,566 --> 00:03:25,466
Axile!

82
00:03:25,145 --> 00:03:28,471
The walls reach the centre,
so the ovary has rooms.

83
00:03:28,471 --> 00:03:30,228
Free central has no walls.

84
00:03:30,878 --> 00:03:31,778
Remember this.

85
00:03:31,735 --> 00:03:33,287
Aestivation folds the petals.

86
00:03:33,287 --> 00:03:34,920
Placentation places the ovules.

87
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:37,237
Now, a worked example.

88
00:03:37,237 --> 00:03:38,954
Six plants, six clues.

89
00:03:38,954 --> 00:03:40,103
Name the type.

90
00:03:40,453 --> 00:03:43,938
Pea petals: a big standard
over two wings and a keel.

91
00:03:43,938 --> 00:03:44,838
Vexillary.

92
00:03:45,159 --> 00:03:48,190
China rose petals overlap one way round.

93
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:49,090
Twisted.

94
00:03:49,446 --> 00:03:51,891
Calotropis petals only touch at the edges.

95
00:03:51,891 --> 00:03:52,791
Valvate.

96
00:03:52,968 --> 00:03:55,954
A pea pod has ovules in
two rows along one seam.

97
00:03:55,954 --> 00:03:56,854
Marginal.

98
00:03:57,090 --> 00:04:01,147
Mustard has ovules on the wall,
and a false wall in the middle.

99
00:04:01,147 --> 00:04:02,047
Parietal.

100
00:04:02,318 --> 00:04:06,098
And a sunflower ovary holds
one ovule at its base.

101
00:04:06,098 --> 00:04:06,998
Basal.

102
00:04:07,436 --> 00:04:08,897
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:09,247 --> 00:04:13,015
Many students think mustard has axile
placentation, because a wall runs

104
00:04:13,015 --> 00:04:14,208
through the middle.

105
00:04:14,608 --> 00:04:18,119
But that wall is a false
wall, called a false septum.

106
00:04:18,119 --> 00:04:22,215
The ovules still grow on the outer
wall, so mustard is parietal.

107
00:04:22,865 --> 00:04:24,096
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:24,096 --> 00:04:28,378
As match the following: a plant with its
aestivation, or with its placentation.

109
00:04:28,978 --> 00:04:30,226
Exam level now.

110
00:04:30,226 --> 00:04:32,934
Match each plant with its placentation.

111
00:04:32,934 --> 00:04:34,236
Pause and solve.

112
00:04:39,636 --> 00:04:40,900
The answer is A.

113
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,558
Pea is marginal, and tomato is axile.

114
00:04:44,058 --> 00:04:46,703
Argemone is parietal,
with ovules on the wall.

115
00:04:46,703 --> 00:04:49,043
Dianthus is free central, with no walls.

116
00:04:49,643 --> 00:04:51,184
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:51,184 --> 00:04:52,768
Screenshot this card.

118
00:04:55,968 --> 00:04:58,154
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:04:58,554 --> 00:04:59,454
One.

120
00:04:58,927 --> 00:05:01,879
What is the difference between
twisted and imbricate aestivation?

121
00:05:02,779 --> 00:05:03,679
Two.

122
00:05:03,203 --> 00:05:05,861
Name the three kinds of
petals in a pea flower.

123
00:05:06,761 --> 00:05:07,661
Three.

124
00:05:07,317 --> 00:05:10,786
Why is mustard parietal, even
with a wall in the middle?

125
00:05:11,886 --> 00:05:15,520
Next time: a mango and a coconut
are the same kind of fruit.

126
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:16,420
How?

127
00:05:15,969 --> 00:05:17,966
We find out in fruits and seeds.
